:\\"Concise four‑event story of the French Revolution causes\\",\\"type\\":\\"story\\",\\"setup\\":\\"A short, chronological narrative linking four main causes: social inequality, economic crisis, political dysfunction, and Enlightenment ideas. Read through each event in order to see how one problem led into the next.\\",\\"events\\":[{\\"id\\":\\"e1\\",\\"label\\":\\"Social inequality and Estate structure (pre-1789)\\",\\"detail\\":\\"France was divided into three Estates: clergy, nobility, and the Third Estate (commoners). The Third Estate bore most taxes and had little political power, creating widespread resentment and a sense of injustice.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"e2\\",\\"label\\":\\"Economic crisis and fiscal strain (late 1780s)\\",\\"detail\\":\\"Years of war and royal spending left the government nearly bankrupt. Poor harvests and rising bread prices hit the Third Estate hardest, turning resentment into acute hardship and increased unrest.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"e3\\",\\"label\\":\\"Political dysfunction and failed reform attempts (1788–1789)\\",\\"detail\\":\\"Pressure for tax reform forced Louis XVI to call the Estates‑General. Political deadlock over voting procedures and privilege blocked meaningful reform, convincing many that the old system could not solve the crisis.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"e4\\",\\"label\\":\\"Spread of Enlightenment ideas and mobilization (throughout the 1780s)\\",\\"detail\\":\\"Enlightenment writers promoted liberty, equality, and popular sovereignty. These ideas gave people language and goals to challenge the ancien régime, so economic pain plus political failure turned into organized action.\\"}],\\"takeaway\\":\\"Sequence: long‑standing social inequality set the moral grievance, economic collapse made suffering immediate, political failure shut down peaceful reform, and Enlightenment ideas provided the framework to turn grievance into revolution.\\"}", "startedAtMs": 25594, "completedAtMs": 25594, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic pr