Peasant's short account of 1789", "blockId": "live_block_2_0450c032", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Peasant's short account of 1789\\",\\"type\\":\\"story\\",\\"setup\\":\\"Speak in a brief first-person voice as a Parisian peasant. Tell four short, chronological events that link major causes of the French Revolution (name at least three causes) and show how one cause helps explain a primary-source detail about bread shortages or taxes.\\",\\"events\\":[{\\"id\\":\\"e1\\",\\"label\\":\\"Winter of hunger and rising bread prices\\",\\"detail\\":\\"I remember the winter when the winter grain failed and bread prices soared; my family skipped meals while taxes still came due. The food shortage and unfair tax burden made daily survival and resentment of the system immediate and personal.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"e2\\",\\"label\\":\\"News of inequality and Enlightenment talk\\",\\"detail\\":\\"At the market I heard educated men and pamphlets saying rulers and nobles lived by privilege while ordinary folk paid for everything. Those Enlightenment ideas and talk of estate inequality made me think the order that let taxes and hunger persist was unjust.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"e3\\",\\"label\\":\\"The fiscal crisis and government debt\\",\\"detail\\":\\"Word spread that the king’s government was nearly bankrupt after costly wars and poor taxation. Officials called a meeting of estates to raise money — that showed the whole system was breaking and that change might be forced from above or below.\\"},{\\"id\\":\\"e4\\",\\"label\\":\\"Uprising mood in the streets\\",\\"detail\\":\\"When people gathered and complained about taxes, bread, and privileges, anger turned public: we joined crowds in Paris demanding lower prices, fairer laws, and an end to privilege. The combination of hunger, visible inequality, and a bankrupt government pushed ordinary folk into action.\\"}],\\"takeaway\\":\\"In four quick events I named three major causes — food shortages/high bread prices, the unequal estates/tax burden, and the fiscal crisis — and linked Enlightenment ideas as a catalyst. To read a primary source about a peasant rioter complaining about ‘bread and taxes,’ interpret it knowing that immediate hunger (food shortage) made grievance urgent, inequality gave it a moral frame, and the fiscal crisis made political change seem possible.\\"}", "startedAtMs": 31414, "completedAtMs": 31414, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile's opening pattern recommends a story after goal framing.", "Stories reveal causal sequencing and applicability of causes in context.", "Limited time — keep the story short and focused (3–4 events).", "Learner persona (fast_confident) responds well to an active, applied prompt."], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Student produces a short (3–4 event) narrative from a specified perspective that names at least three causes, sequences them causally, and briefly applies one cause to interpret a provided primary-source excerpt or hypothetical scenario.", "firstContentAtMs": 31414, "studentSafeReason": "A brief story lets the student apply causes in context without requiring extended writing; it's low-risk and reveals how well they can sequence and connect causes.", "studentConsentRequired": false}, {"id": "live_move_3_f2f601d9", "type": "contrast_case", "phase": "active", "title": "Contrast: Which scenario is more likely to spark revolution?", "blockId": "live_block_3_a4dd5f32", "content": "{\\"schemaVersion\\":\\"petros.render.v2\\",\\"title\\":\\"Contrast: Which scenario is more likely to spark revolution?\\",\\"type\\":\\"contrast_case\\",\\"left\\":{\\"label\\":\\"Scenario A — Rural crisis and food shortages\\",\\"prompt\\":\\"A long winter destroys grain harvests across the countryside. Bread prices triple. Local peasants face rising rents and unpaid taxes while provincial nobles demand fealty and fines. Rumors of hoarded grain spread to nearby towns.\\",\\"method\\":\\"List which classic causes of the French Revolution appear here (economic hardship, food scarcity, regressive taxation, elite extraction, political exclusion). Explain which of those causes are present and why they matter. Decide whether this combination is likely sufficient to provoke widespread unrest leading toward revolution, and justify your judgment with at least three connected reasons.\\"},\\"right\\":{\\"label\\":\\"Scenario B — Urban elite crisis and court extravagance\\",\\"prompt\\":\\"Parisian salons and court life remain opulent while the crown borrows heavily to fund foreign wars. A new tax reform bill is proposed but blocked by aristocratic privilege. Urban lawyers and bourgeois professionals publish pamphlets demanding legal and fiscal equality.\\",\\"method\\":\\"Identify which revolution causes appear here (fiscal crisis, ineffective reform, elite resistance, rising political consciousness, urban mobilization). Explain which causes are present and why they matter. Judge whether this set of causes is likely sufficient to provoke revolutionary upheaval, giving at least three connected reasons.\\"},\\"keyDistinction\\":\\"Compare which scenario is more likely to produce revolutionary upheaval by naming at least three causes present or absent in each, and explaining how their combination affects the likelihood (e.g., food shortages mobilize broad popular support; elite resistance plus urban political networks can turn reform failures into radical change).\\"}", "startedAtMs": 46949, "completedAtMs": 46949, "sourceAnchors": [], "decisionFactors": ["Topic profile recommends contrast_case after a story for depth evaluation.", "Contrast cases reveal whether the student can distinguish which causes are necessary or sufficient.", "Limited time and one move remaining — a brief contrast is an efficient high-evidence probe.", "Learner persona (fast_confident) benefits from a challenge that requires quick reasoning and judgment."], "artifactProposed": false, "expectedEvidence": "Student compares two short scenarios and explains which one is more likely to lead to revolutionary unrest, naming at least three causes and justifying which causes are present/absent and why that matters.", "firstContentAtMs": 46949, "studentSafeReason": "A contrast case elicits application and discrimination of causes without requiring long production; it's low-risk and fits the evaluation flow.", "studentConsentRequired": false}], "runId": "petros-stage5-live-smoke-v1", "usage": [{"source": "evaluation", "retries": 0, "cacheHit": false, "modelKey": "gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07", "fallbacks": 0, "latencyMs": 10006, "requestId": "8f59368f-fc3a-4430-8ba0-e143025aa00a", "costMicros": 2637, "functionKey": "teaching.next_action.select", "inputTokens": 21750, "providerKey": "openai-live-eval", "outputTokens": 875, "validationFailures": 0}, {"source": "evaluation", "retries": 0, "cacheHit": false, "modelKey": "gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07", "fallbacks": 0, "latencyMs": 3471, "requestId": "7bae0421-de1a-4404-b20d-d0999bbab17d", "costMicros": 1069, "functionKey": "teaching.block.render", "inputTokens": 5829, "providerKey": "openai-live-eval", "outputTokens": 238, "validationFailures": 0}, {"source": "evaluation", "retries": 0, "cacheHit": false, "modelKey": "gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07", "fallbacks": 0, "latencyMs": 10886, "requestId": "9799b1e8-5aa9-4f90-b102-4afa41319a08", "costMicros": 2791, "functionKey": "teaching.next_action.select", "inputTokens": 22254, "providerKey": "openai-live-eval", "outputTokens": 889, "validationFailures": 0}, {"source": "evaluation", "retries": 0, "cacheHit": false, "modelKey": "gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07", "fallbacks": 0, "latencyMs": 7010, "requestId": "0450c032-8f4e-4b78-bf46-be2cf393234c", "costMicros": 2003, "functionKey": "teaching.block.render", "inputTokens": 6340, "providerKey": "openai-live-eval", "outputTokens": 641, "validationFailures": 0}, {"source": "evaluation", "retries": 0, "cacheHit": false, "modelKey": "gpt-5-mini-2025-08-07", "fallba