₂ (e.g., C-14) show up in plant sugars, proving atmospheric CO₂ is the carbon source for glucose.", "Oxygen measurements: plants release O₂ in light but not in the dark, linking light-driven reactions to the production of chemical energy and breakdown of CO₂/H₂O into sugars and O₂.", "Cell biology: chloroplasts (seen in microscopy) contain the pigments and enzymes that use light energy to build sugars from CO₂ and H₂O."], "correction": "Not exactly. Most of the matter in plant sugar (glucose) comes from carbon dioxide (CO₂) and water (H₂O). Using light energy captured by chlorophyll inside chloroplasts, plants assemble carbon and hydrogen from CO₂ and H₂O into glucose and release O₂. Soil supplies water and dissolved mineral nutrients (like nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium) that support growth, but it does not provide the carbon atoms that make up sugars.", "misconception": "Plants obtain their food directly from soil.", "schemaVersion": "petros.render.v2", "commitmentPrompt": "Before I explain, do you agree with this statement: \\"Plants get the food they need directly from soil\\"? Say 'Agree' or 'Disagree'."}, "version": 0, "whyThis": "This optional voice session needs a concise, speakable misconception demolition with the complete equivalent kept in text.", "delivery": "ai", "sequence": 2, "createdAt": "2026-08-14T18:17:52.332Z", "questions": [{"id": "ee123fab-af95-41e0-9fab-a64df2748a9d", "hints": [], "active": true, "locked": false, "prompt": "Before I explain, do you agree with this statement: \\"Plants get the food they need directly from soil\\"? Say 'Agree' or 'Disagree'.", "choices": [{"id": "agree", "label": "Agree"}, {"id": "disagree", "label": "Disagree"}, {"id": "unsure", "label": "Not sure yet"}], "purpose": "guess_first", "attempts": [], "targetId": "checkpoint-2:photosynthesis:misconception_demolition:misconception-commitment", "responseType": "single_choice", "equivalenceKey": "6afd5dab5b96fcc62f74b08574047764d2f110364f3823e9", "rootQuestionId": "e