apture important patterns. A perfectly fit decision tree performs well for training data but performs poorly for unseen test data. If the decision tree is allowed to train to its full strength, the mode", "section": "Pages 25–27", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 28, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 28–30)", "excerpt": "Another com plete solved example can be foun d at - https://kindsonthegenius.com/blog/how - to - build - a - decision - tree - for - classification - step - by - step - procedure - using - entrop y - and - gain/ Visit www.sharikatr.in for my notes and ppts So 𝐺𝑎𝑖𝑛 𝑆, 𝐴 1 = ES − { 𝑆 𝐴1=1 𝑆 𝐸𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑜𝑝𝑦 𝑆 𝐴1=1 + 𝑆 𝐴1=0 𝑆 𝐸𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑜𝑝𝑦 𝑆 𝐴1=0 } = 1 − 3 6 𝑋0 .9183 + 3 6 𝑋 0.9183 = 0.0817 ----- (1) DEPARTMENT OF CSE SNGCE 68 |S A2=1 |=4 Entropy(S A2=1 )= -P 1 log 2 P 1 - P 0 log 2 P 0 = − 2 4 𝑙𝑜𝑔 2 2 4 − 2 4 𝑙𝑜𝑔 2 2 4 = 1 |S A2=0 |=2 Entropy(S A1=0 )= − 1 2 𝑙𝑜𝑔 2 1 2 − 1 2 𝑙𝑜𝑔 2 1 2 = 1 DEPARTMENT OF CSE SNGCE 69 X A2 Y X1 1 1 X2 1 1 X5 1 0 X6 1 0 X A2 Y X3 0 0 X4 0 1 S A2=1 S A2=0 Visit https://www.youtube.com/c/sharikatr for my video lectures Visit www.sharikatr.in for my notes and ppts Visit www.sharikatr.in for my notes and ppts So 𝐺𝑎𝑖𝑛 𝑆, 𝐴 2 = ES − { 𝑆 𝐴2=1 𝑆 𝐸𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑜𝑝𝑦 𝑆 𝐴2=1 + 𝑆 𝐴2=0 𝑆 𝐸𝑛𝑡𝑟𝑜𝑝𝑦 𝑆 𝐴2=0 } = 1 − 4 6 𝑋 1 + 2 6 𝑋 1 = 0 ---------- (2) DEPARTMENT OF CSE SNGCE 70 From (1) and (2) We found that A1 give more information gain. So we make A1 as root node DEPARTMENT OF CSE SNGCE 71 A1 A2 1 0 A2 1 1 0 0 1 0 0 1 X A2", "section": "Pages 28–30", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}, {"page": 31, "label": "Machine Learning — Module 5: Learning from Examples (p. 31–33)", "excerpt": "From error rates to loss • So far, we have been trying to minimize error rate. This is clearly better than maximizing error rate, this is not enough, • Consider the problem of classifying email messages as spam or non - spam. It is worse to classify non - spam as spam (and thus potentially miss an importa nt message) then to classify spam as non - spam (and thus suffer a few seconds of annoyance). So a classifier with a 1% error rate, where almost all the errors were classifying spam as non - spam, would be better than a classifier with only a 0.5% error rate, if most of those errors were classifying non - spam as spam. decision - makers should maximize expected utility, and utility is what learners should maximize as well. In 696 Chapter 18. Learning from Examples (c) (a) (b) (d) x x x x f ( x ) f ( x ) f ( x ) f ( x ) Figure 18.1 (a) Example ( x, f ( x )) pairs and a consistent, linear hypothesis. (b) A con- sistent, degree-7 polynomial hypothesis for the same data set. (c) A different data set, which admits an exact degree-6 polynomial fit or an approximate linear fit. (d) A simple, exact sinusoidal fit to the same data set. generalizes well if it correctly predicts the value of", "section": "Pages 31–33", "version": "1:46b63aeb22c661ff", "sourceId": "136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "sourceType": "document"}], "createdAt": "2026-09-01T18:26:06.011Z", "expiresAt": "2026-09-01T18:56:06.007Z", "studentId": "0cd6f10d-fa83-4df4-a3b4-5d9d202aace5", "sessionType": "learn", "accessibility": [], "correlationId": "f8babd05-6d1f-4541-a2a1-e4a21bd183c4", "institutionId": "dc099dcb-289c-476b-99ed-d29d486b7c7b", "schemaVersion": "petros.session-intent.v1", "idempotencyKey": "9572f327-f245-4e71-96b5-8d8c7fe0c642", "targetConcepts": ["Pages 1–3", "Pages 4–6", "Pages 7–9", "Pages 10–12", "Pages 13–15", "Pages 16–18", "Pages 19–21", "Pages 22–24", "Pages 25–27", "Pages 28–30", "Pages 31–33"], "homeworkContext": null, "registerFactors": ["The student explicitly asked to be taught from their own uploaded pages.", "Keep source grounding visible and separate explanation from quotation."], "sourceEntityIds": ["136cd389-8be9-4f12-937f-8bcbc5acf96a", "ece08150-f9d5-491